Important: The port binding `127.0.0.1:3306:3306` allows connections from applications running directly on your Debian server. It does not expose MySQL to other computers.

If you want to connect from your Ubuntu laptop or another computer on your LAN, change it to:

```
ports:
  - "3306:3306"
```

This exposes the port on all host interfaces, so restrict TCP port 3306 with your firewall to trusted LAN IP addresses only. Do not expose it to the public internet.

## 4. Start MySQL

Run:

```
docker compose up -d
```

Docker will download the MySQL image if necessary, create the volume, and start the database container.

Check its status:

```
docker ps
```

Check startup logs:

```
docker logs -f mysql-server
```

The initial database creation may take some time. Wait until MySQL reports that it is ready for connections.

## 5. Enter MySQL inside the container

To open the MySQL command-line client:

```
docker exec -it mysql-server mysql -u root -p
```

Enter your root password when prompted.

You should see:

```
mysql>
```

Now run some SQL commands:

```
SHOW DATABASES;

USE student_db;

CREATE TABLE students (
    id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    age INT
);

INSERT INTO students (name, age)
VALUES ('Alice', 20);

SELECT * FROM students;
```

You should get a result similar to:

```
+----+-------+------+
| id | name  | age  |
+----+-------+------+
|  1 | Alice |   20 |
+----+-------+------+
```

Exit MySQL:

```
exit;
```

## 6. Connect your local applications

The connection address depends on where your application runs.

| Application location        | MySQL host                                 | Port                         |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------- |
| On Debian host              | `127.0.0.1`                                | 3306                         |
| On another LAN computer     | Debian server's LAN IP                     | 3306                         |
| In the same Compose project | `mysql`                                    | 3306                         |
| In another Docker project   | Shared Docker network name or host address | Depends on connection method |

For example, if your Debian server's LAN address is `192.168.1.114`, an Ubuntu application would use:

```
Host:     192.168.1.114
Port:     3306
Database: student_db
Username: student_app
Password: your_app_password
```

The `192.168.1.114` address is an example; verify your server's current address using:

```
hostname -I
```

### Connect from a Java Spring Boot application

Since you're developing a Java student management system using Spring Boot and Hibernate, here is a practical `application.properties` example.

```
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://192.168.1.114:3306/student_db
spring.datasource.username=student_app
spring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}

spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.show-sql=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.dialect=org.hibernate.dialect.MySQLDialect
```

Set `DB_PASSWORD` in your application's environment to the password you chose for `MYSQL_PASSWORD`.

You'll also need the MySQL JDBC driver dependency in Maven:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.mysql</groupId>
    <artifactId>mysql-connector-j</artifactId>
    <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
```

For local development, `ddl-auto=update` can be convenient. For production, use database migrations such as Flyway or Liquibase instead.

## 7. Manage your MySQL container

These are the Docker commands you'll use frequently:

| Task                  | Command                                         |
| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Check status          | `docker ps -a`                                  |
| Stop MySQL            | `docker stop mysql-server`                      |
| Start MySQL           | `docker start mysql-server`                     |
| Restart MySQL         | `docker restart mysql-server`                   |
| View logs             | `docker logs mysql-server`                      |
| Enter container shell | `docker exec -it mysql-server bash`             |
| Enter MySQL client    | `docker exec -it mysql-server mysql -u root -p` |

The database is stored in the `mysql_data` Docker volume. It survives normal container restarts and recreation. Avoid `docker compose down -v` because it removes the volume and its data. Also back up the database regularly.

Note that `.env` initialization values only apply when the database is first created. Changing a password in `.env` later does not change the existing MySQL user's password.&#x20;

